    "text_delta": "ing the decision.\n* Panel members are also balancing heavy teaching, research, and grading loads.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Do not bring up the gender of the panel or the candidate:** Framing your inquiry around gender will close doors and put the department on the defensive.\n2. **Follow up politely and professionally:** If another week passes (around 3 to 4 weeks total), send a brief, courteous follow-up to the search chair:\n   > *\"Dear Professor [Name], I hope your semester is going well. I am following up on my previous note regarding feedback on my interview for the lecturer position. I appreciated the opportunity to meet the department and would welcome any brief advice or observations you might have on how I can strengthen my candidacy for similar roles in the future. Thank you again for your time and consideration.\"*\n3. **Frame it around professional growth:** Keep the tone forward-looking. Search chairs are far more willing to offer candid, helpful advice to candidates who show grace and a desire to improve than to candidates who appear to be challenging the verdict.\n4. **Channel your energy into the next search:** Having a finished PhD, six peer-reviewed articles, and four years of teaching experience makes you a competitive candidate. Reaching the final three and completing two rounds of interviews proves that your profile is attractive to hiring committees. Focus on refining your interview presentations and identifying departments where your specific skill set is the exact fit they need.\n",
