    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "It is completely understandable to feel frustrated and look for explanations when you lose out on a position where you feel your credentials—such as publications, degree status, and teaching experience—were stronger on paper. \n\nHowever, **making an issue out of the panel's gender composition or assuming it explains the outcome would almost certainly be a mistake and work against your professional interests.**\n\nHere is a breakdown of why, along with what usually drives decisions like this in academic hiring and how best to proceed:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Does an all-women panel indicate bias?\nOn its own, **no.** \n\n* **How panels are formed:** Hiring committees at public universities are usually assembled based on administrative roles (e.g., department head, program director, search chair), subject area representation, and service obligations. An all-women panel is often simply the result of who holds those specific roles or who was assigned to committee service that semester.\n* **Lack of evidentiary basis:** In law and university policy, panel demographics alone do not constitute evidence of bias. Assuming or asserting that a female panel chose a female candidate out of favoritism is an assumption without factual basis, and raising it without concrete proof of procedural irregularity or overt discrimination will almost certainly be viewed as an unfounded grievance.\n* **Reputational risk:** Academic disciplines (including economics) are tight-knit. Making an accusation of gender bias based purely on the panel's makeup will likely damage your professional reputation among colleagues, future hiring committees, and external reviewers far more than losing this particular job.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Why might the other candidate have been selected?\nWhen candidates reach the final shortlist of three, the raw metrics on a CV (number of publications or years of teaching) often take a back seat. Final decisions typically hinge on factors that are not evident from an announcement:\n\n* **Role-specific needs:** For a *lecturer* position, departments often prioritize teaching versatility, pedagogical innovation, or connections to applied fields over raw publication counts. If the department wants to build industry ties, internships, or practical curriculum, \"strong industry experience\" can be a major deciding factor.\n* **Sub-field alignment:** Economics encompasses widely varying specializations (e.g., microeconometrics, macroeconomics, finance, behavioral, labor, health). The other candidate’s specific niche or research pipeline may have directly filled a specific curricular or departmental gap.\n* **Interview and job talk performance:** The campus visit, teaching demonstration, and interviews carry immense weight. How candidates engage with students, interact with colleagues, and articulate a vision for the courses often outweighs small differences in paper counts.\n* **Trajectory and potential:** A candidate near completion of a PhD with industry experience and high-quality early publications is often viewed as having strong momentum and unique perspectives to bring into the classroom.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The two-week silence on feedback\nTwo weeks without a response is standard—and often fast—by academic administration standards:\n* Hiring searches frequently require sign-offs from HR, the dean's office, and compliance before any formal candidate communications or feedback can be issued.\n* Until the winning candidate has formally signed their contract, the search remains technically open, and committees are often legally or procedurally barred from discussing the decision.\n* Panel members are also balancing heavy teaching, research, and grading loads.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Do not bring up the gender of the panel or the candidate:** Framing your inquiry around gender will close doors and put the department on the defensive.\n2. **Follow up politely and professionally:** If another week passes (around 3 to 4 weeks total), send a brief, courteous follow-up to the search chair:\n   > *\"Dear Professor [Name], I hope your semester is going well. I am following up on my previous note regarding feedback on my interview for the lecturer position. I appreciated the opportunity to meet the department and would welcome any brief advice or observations you might have on how I can strengthen my candidacy for similar roles in the future. Thank you again for your time and consideration.\"*\n3. **Frame it around professional growth:** Keep the tone forward-looking. Search chairs are far more willing to offer candid, helpful advice to candidates who show grace and a desire to improve than to candidates who appear to be challenging the verdict.\n4. **Channel your energy into the next search:** Having a finished PhD, six peer-reviewed articles, and four years of teaching experience makes you a competitive candidate. Reaching the final three and completing two rounds of interviews proves that your profile is attractive to hiring committees. Focus on refining your interview presentations and identifying departments where your specific skill set is the exact fit they need.\n",
